WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.300
Aside from all the weird aliens, roguish bounty
hunters and thrilling action sequences, one

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.360
thing Disney's flagship series The Mandalorian
has in common with the Star Wars saga is that

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:14.559
it's chock-full of subtle references and callbacks
to other Star Wars media.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:16.520
Here are a few you may have missed.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.980
As you'd hope from any decent Star Wars story,
The Mandalorian features all kinds of different

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.640
alien races and cultures.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.720
Take that first bar scene, for example.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:30.160
During this scene, a fearsome-looking man
with an unruly beard beats up on a blue-skinned

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.880
Mythrol, the bounty the Mandalorian is after.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.580
This guy then tries to pick a fight with the
Mandalorian himself.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.220
But here's a bonus, for you: the language
he's speaking is Huttese, the language of

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:59.020
the Hutts, which is used most notably by Jabba
in Return of the Jedi.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:11.980
After the Mandalorian captures and carts off
the Mythrol to collect the price on his head,

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:16.120
the prisoner makes small talk with the stoic,
silent bounty hunter back on his ship.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.820
"Yeah, I was hoping to be free for Life Day…"

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.240
So what's Life Day?

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:27.120
Well, it's the Star Wars universe's equivalent
of Christmas, a time for celebrating and relaxing

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:27.940
with loved ones.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:32.700
It was first introduced in The Star Wars Holiday
Special, an infamous Star Wars-themed television

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:33.700
variety show.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:38.140
The Special follows Chewbacca's family as
they watch futuristic TV and wait for him

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:39.410
to arrive for Life Day.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.540
It even ends with Princess Leia singing a
Life Day carol.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:43.340
Ugh.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.720
Life Day isn't the only Mandalorian callback
to that unloved Star Wars Holiday Special.

00:01:48.729 --> 00:01:53.060
The show's protagonist also sports a distinctive
weapon that should be familiar to longtime

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.560
fans of a galaxy far, far away.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:00.030
It's a cool-looking apparatus with a heck
of a past: in Boba Fett's first-ever appearance,

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:04.000
during an animated sequence in the Holiday
Special, he's packing the same weapon.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:08.340
It likely doesn't mean much as far as a connection
to Fett is concerned, but it's a nifty nod

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:12.970
to Star Wars lore that proves showrunner Jon
Favreau is deeply invested in layering fan

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:14.580
service into his series.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.420
Early on in the episode, when the Mandalorian
and his fin-faced bounty are booking passage

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:23.510
back to his ship, they encounter an alien
hidden behind a pair of goggles and a long

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:24.000
snout.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:28.240
He plays a killer flute, and his name is never
revealed, but he should've been instantly

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:31.280
recognizable to any detail-oriented Star Wars
fan.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:36.230
This fella is a Kubaz, an alien species who's
only shown up one other time over the course

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:37.480
of the Star Wars saga.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:41.840
Cast your mind back to A New Hope, specifically
the scene shortly after Luke and Obi-Wan meet

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.360
Han Solo and Chewbacca.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:48.560
In the alleyways of Mos Eisley, a Kubaz named
Garindan contacts the Imperial Stormtroopers

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:52.360
patrolling the streets to let them know that
he's found the droids they're looking for.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:57.080
While it's pretty unlikely that this guy will
show up in any other episodes of The Mandalorian,

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:00.560
it's still a pretty cool deep cut into the
galaxy's expansive mythos.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:05.500
Of course, it goes without saying that The
Mandalorian is full to the brim of droids.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:09.020
In one particular sequence, the Mandalorian
walks through a marketplace.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:13.050
One of the objects he walks by is some kind
of red and white R5 droid, which looks an

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:17.379
awful lot like that red and white R5-D4 drone
from A New Hope, you know, the one with the

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:18.380
"bad motivator."

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:26.680
"Uncle Owen, this droid has a bad motivator unit! Look!"

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:31.320
When the Mandalorian arrives at the fortified
headquarters of "The Client," he's also greeted

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:35.740
by a round, red-light equipped droid tasked
with security and gatekeeping tasks.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:39.820
And sure enough, that little fella looks just
like the security droid Jabba the Hutt had

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:42.960
installed on the front gate of his palace
in Return of the Jedi.

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:48.420
In The Mandalorian, times seem to be especially
tough for the Empire and its many underlings.

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:51.830
For example, the man initially known only
as "The Client" appears to be an ex-Imperial,

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:56.440
something he's not exactly afraid to flaunt,
as he surrounds himself with a squad of those

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:58.480
familiar white-armored stormtroopers.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:02.319
They're still pretty formidable looking, but
they've clearly fallen on hard times since

00:04:02.319 --> 00:04:07.150
the end of the Empire, reflected by the fact
that their outfits are well-worn, a bit dirty,

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:08.680
and even cracked in some parts.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:13.380
Perpetually-masked bounty hunter Boba Fett
is probably one of the most treasured characters

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:18.209
in all of Star Wars...but he's actually not
the Mandalorian, in fact, he's not a Mandalorian

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:18.780
at all.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:19.740
Confused?

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:24.759
Luckily, the show appears set to teach casual
Star Wars fans a little more about the Mandalorians

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:25.480
as a culture.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:29.410
Not much is revealed in that first episode,
but the protagonist does venture into some

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:32.040
sort of special forge in order to upgrade
his armor.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:36.540
Carrying a slab of Beskar steel that he acquired
from "The Client," he descends into an area

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:41.190
where numerous other Mandalorians are shown,
including one shadowy figure in the background

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:43.900
who looks suspiciously like the long-dead
Boba Fett.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:48.130
It's also in this scene that the show offers
a few brief glimpses into the Mandalorian's

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:52.880
past; specifically, the attack that orphaned
him as a boy and left him a "foundling," the

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:53.860
Mandalorian term for an orphan.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:58.410
The forger who makes his new pauldron mentions
that the leftover scrap will be used to sponsor

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:03.020
other foundlings, the audience's first clue
that, in addition to being a fearsome bounty

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:05.960
hunter, this character may also be pursuing
a nobler agenda.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:11.520
Until now, the Kowakian monkey-lizard has
had just one place in Star Wars canon: as

00:05:11.521 --> 00:05:14.690
the snickering pet of unsavory characters
like Jabba the Hutt.

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:18.970
They're mischievous, they're obnoxious, they
seem well-suited for villainy...and, apparently,

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:19.740
they're delicious.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:24.080
Immediately after the Mandalorian gets his
bounty assignment from "The Client," he walks

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:28.560
through the marketplace of a desolate-looking
little town in which one familiar alien species

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:29.300
stands out.

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:33.540
Sucks for him, too, because he's being spit-roasted
while his horrified buddy looks on.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:37.940
Honestly, this is a fairly horrific moment,
especially when you remember that these animals

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:40.870
are intelligent enough to have a pretty well-developed
sense of humor.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:43.200
But hey, that's the outer rim for you.

